-- title:
--   Proposition 4.12: Euler elements centralize the radical
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak g$ be admissible and non-reductive, with $\mathfrak z(\mathfrak g)\subseteq[\mathfrak g,\mathfrak g]$. If $h$ is an Euler element, meaning that $\operatorname{ad}(h)$ is diagonalizable with weights among $-1,0,1$, then for the solvable radical $\mathfrak r$,
--
--   $$[h,\mathfrak r]=0.$$
--
--   This is centralization of the radical, not vanishing of every Euler element.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 24, Proposition 4.12: Euler elements centralize the radical

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Frontier30_P26

